content.strength_and_weaknesses:
Strenghts:
* Methods were benchmarked across 9 different datasets
* The novel architecture introduced with CGRAN is technically interesting and has the potential to yield new biological insights in applications to scRNA-seq datasets.

Major concerns:
* Comparison with other methods. Specifically:
  * Many of the methods considered in the comparison paper Abdelaal et al (2019) are missing;
  * It is my understanding that all the methods were applied considering the top 1,000 variable genes only.
However, some of the baseline methods consider a different gene/feature selection procedure (for example, ACTINN “uses all the genes to capture the features for each cell type” [1]). This could lead to suboptimal results in the author’s implementation;
  * If computationally feasible, it would be important to quantify how variable are the reported accuracy for different train/test splits of the data (e.g., quantifying mean and standard deviations of accuracy across splits);
* Claims on model interpretability. I think the authors would need to show more data to demonstrate their claims about model interpretability. For example, it is true that in CGRAN gene-by-gene self-attention can lead to the identification of gene sets, but the authors do not demonstrate that the model is able to do so (just provide a single example). Similarly, the claim that highly differential genes are good marker genes should be further supported;
* Reproducibility. The proposed model has many hyperparameters but the authors do not comment on how they were chosen.

[1] Ma F, Pellegrini M. ACTINN: automated identification of cell types in single cell RNA sequencing. Bioinformatics. 2020 Jan 15;36(2):533-8.

content.summary_of_the_review:
Overall, I think the models introduced by the authors are interesting and have the potential to yield new biological insights in applications to scRNA-seq datasets. However, more data is needed to demonstrate their improved performance over the state-of-the-art and to support their claims on model interpretability.

Specific comments (also discussed in weaknesses):
* Many of the methods considered in the comparison paper Abdelaal et al (2019) are missing. I think the authors should either add more methods or clearly justify why their selection is satisfactory;
* The authors considered the top 1,000 variable genes. Was this feature set used for other methods? If yes, how do the results change if the authors consider the same feature selection procedure considered in their original implementation?
* The authors should quantify variability on reported accuracies across different train/test splits;
* Additional data to prove model interpretability (see weaknesses). I think current data just support that the model behaves as expected;
* Please clarify hyperparameter selection.
